---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def from '../calculators/square-footage';
import { ft2To } from '../lib/units';
import { fmt } from '../lib/format';

const rooms: [number, number][] = [
  [10, 10],
  [10, 12],
  [12, 12],
  [12, 14],
  [12, 16],
  [14, 16],
  [16, 20],
  [20, 20],
];
---

<CalcPage
  slug="square-footage-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="Square Footage Calculator: Feet & Inches, Any Shape"
  description="Calculate square feet from feet and inches for rectangles, circles, and triangles. Add rooms to a project and convert to square yards, meters, or acres."
  h1="Square Footage Calculator"
  updated="2026-11-08"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Find the area of a room, floor, yard, or lot in square feet, with measurements in feet and inches. Add several areas to
    a project for L-shaped rooms or a whole house, and convert to square yards, square meters, or acres.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="how-to">How to calculate square footage</h2>
  <p>
    For a rectangle, multiply length by width in feet: a 12 × 15 ft room is <strong>180 square feet</strong>. For a
    circle, multiply π by the radius squared. For a triangle, multiply the base by the height and divide by 2.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Shape</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Rectangle or square</td><td><code>length × width</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Circle</td><td><code>π × (diameter ÷ 2)²</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Triangle</td><td><code>base × height ÷ 2</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="feet-and-inches">How to calculate square feet with feet and inches</h2>
  <p>
    Convert the inches to a decimal of a foot by dividing by 12, then multiply. A room that measures 10 ft 9 in by
    12 ft 3 in is 10.75 × 12.25 = <strong>131.69 square feet</strong>. In the calculator, choose <em>ft + in</em> as the
    unit and type the inches in the second box.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keep feet and inches separate when measuring: 10 ft 9 in is 10.75 ft, not 10.9 ft. Measure to the finished inside
    edges for a flooring estimate, and use the same boundary for every section. This tool calculates from the dimensions
    you enter; it does not read an uploaded floor plan or certify a home's listing area.
  </p>

  <h2 id="irregular">Irregular and L-shaped rooms</h2>
  <p>
    Split the space into rectangles, work out each one, and add them. An L-shaped room made of a 12 × 15 ft section and an
    8 × 6 ft alcove is 180 + 48 = 228 square feet. Use <strong>Add to project</strong> to keep a running total, and add a
    triangle or circle for angled walls and bay windows.
  </p>
  <p>
    Draw the dividing line on your sketch so the rectangles meet without overlapping. For the example above, enter
    12 × 15 ft, select <strong>Add to project</strong>, then enter 8 × 6 ft and add that section. The project total is
    228 ft²; do not also add the outer bounding rectangle, which would count part of the floor twice.
  </p>

  <h2 id="area-or-order">Room area or material to buy?</h2>
  <p>
    The measured area is the starting point for an order. A 12 × 15 ft room is 180 ft²; with an illustrative 10% cutting
    allowance, the purchase estimate is 198 ft². If each flooring box covers 20 ft², round 198 ÷ 20 up to 10 boxes.
    Use the <a href="/flooring-calculator/">flooring box calculator</a> with the product's actual coverage, or the
    <a href="/carpet-calculator/">carpet measurement calculator</a> for square yards and roll-width estimates.
    Apply waste once, after adding the measured sections.
  </p>

  <h2 id="room-sizes">Square footage of common room sizes</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Room size</th><th class="num">Square feet</th><th class="num">Square yards</th><th class="num">Square meters</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          rooms.map(([l, w]) => (
            <tr><td>{l} × {w} ft</td><td class="num">{l * w}</td><td class="num">{fmt(ft2To(l * w, 'yd2'), 1)}</td><td class="num">{fmt(ft2To(l * w, 'm2'), 1)}</td></tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="conversions">Square foot conversions</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>To convert</th><th>Do this</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Square feet → square yards</td><td>Divide by 9</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Square feet → square meters</td><td>Multiply by 0.0929</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Square meters → square feet</td><td>Multiply by 10.764</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Square feet → acres</td><td>Divide by 43,560</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Buying for the room? Take the area to the <a href="/flooring-calculator/">flooring calculator</a> for boxes,
    <a href="/tile-calculator/">the tile calculator</a> for tile counts, <a href="/carpet-calculator/">the carpet calculator</a> for square yards, or
    <a href="/wallpaper-calculator/">work out wallpaper rolls</a> for the walls.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>How many square feet are in an acre?</h3>
  <p>43,560 square feet, about the size of a 208 × 208 ft square.</p>
  <h3>How do I find the square footage of a house?</h3>
  <p>
    Measure each room or section of finished living space and add them up. Appraisers generally measure finished area
    from the outside walls and leave out garages, unfinished basements, and open porches, so your room-by-room total will
    come in a little lower than a listing.
  </p>
  <h3>How much extra should I buy for flooring?</h3>
  <p>
    Add 5–10% for straight layouts and 10–15% for diagonal or herringbone patterns. Pouring a slab instead? Take the area
    to the <a href="/concrete-calculator/">concrete calculator</a>; covering a yard, try the
    <a href="/sod-calculator/">sod calculator</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every measurement is converted to feet before multiplying, so you can mix units.</li>
    <li>Project totals add the square footage of each saved area.</li>
    <li>Conversions: 1 yd² = 9 ft², 1 m² = 10.7639 ft², 1 acre = 43,560 ft².</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
